WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.428
- We're back with the stars and director

00:00:02.428 --> 00:00:05.276
of the new Netflix movie "The
School for Good and Evil".

00:00:05.276 --> 00:00:08.272
Sofia Wylie, Sophia Anne
Caruso, Charlize Theron,

00:00:08.272 --> 00:00:10.605
Kerry Washington, Paul Feig.

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:13.602
It's a lot of people there, I love it.

00:00:13.602 --> 00:00:14.687
And we also have an audience

00:00:14.687 --> 00:00:16.751
full of good and evil characters.

00:00:16.751 --> 00:00:19.438
(audience cheers)

00:00:19.438 --> 00:00:22.639
So, Paul, nice to see you again.

00:00:22.639 --> 00:00:23.472
- Good to see you again. -
[Kelly] This time, in person.

00:00:23.472 --> 00:00:24.305
- I know, exactly. Not over Zoom.

00:00:24.305 --> 00:00:25.138
- [Kelly] Yes, you look...

00:00:25.138 --> 00:00:25.971
- And last time I talked
to you I was in Belfast

00:00:25.971 --> 00:00:27.537
- [Kelly] Great over zoom.
- shooting this movie.

00:00:27.537 --> 00:00:28.370
- I know, I know.

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:29.925
And you looked fabulous then.
- Thanks.

00:00:29.925 --> 00:00:30.758
- You look fabulous now.

00:00:30.758 --> 00:00:31.591
- Why, thank you.

00:00:31.591 --> 00:00:33.420
- So you directed movies
with strong female casts,

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.039
like "Bridesmaids," "Ghostbusters."

00:00:35.039 --> 00:00:36.042
(audience cheers)

00:00:36.042 --> 00:00:38.700
I mean, now this one.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.320
So, I assume that's not a coincidence.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:41.880
Did you have like, strong female,

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.590
like, empowering people
in your life growing up?

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:46.484
- Yeah, I mean most of my
friends growing up were girls,

00:00:46.484 --> 00:00:48.420
and women, as I grew up.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:51.120
And I was an only child,
really close to my mom.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.760
And I just got tired of watching movies

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.160
where women had one
dimensional characters,

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:57.120
especially comedies.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:57.953
- We were too.

00:00:57.953 --> 00:00:59.160
- [Paul] Yeah, exactly.
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:00.180
- Well, especially when I got to Hollywood

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:01.710
and I became friends
with so many, you know,

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:04.590
comedic actresses and
just, you know, actors.

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:06.120
And they were so great.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.100
I'd see them in a movie and
they were playing, you know,

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.230
the shrewish girlfriend
or the terrible wife,

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:11.400
and not getting to be funny

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:12.720
and not getting to have a good role.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:13.830
And I was just like, I hate this.

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:15.942
And I love telling women's
stories anyways, so it's just,

00:01:15.942 --> 00:01:18.330
there's nothing else I
wanna do in my career.

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:19.530
- Well, you're killing it.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:20.363
- [Paul] Oh, thank you.

00:01:20.363 --> 00:01:22.350
(audience applauds)

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:23.703
Look at who I get to work
with though, you know.

00:01:23.703 --> 00:01:24.830
- [Kelly] It's an amazing cast.

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:25.663
- Yeah.

00:01:25.663 --> 00:01:27.121
- Job well done casting, whoever did that.

00:01:27.121 --> 00:01:28.800
How was it working with Paul?

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.570
You said you'd worked with
Paul before, obviously,

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:31.403
so you'd worked with him before.

00:01:31.403 --> 00:01:32.280
But how is it working with him,

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.350
different than maybe other directors?

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:37.890
- He's effortlessly collaborative.

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:38.730
- [Kelly] Oh, that's awesome.

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:41.130
- Yeah, I sometimes as an actor,

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:43.173
I feel like I'm putting everybody out.

00:01:43.173 --> 00:01:44.640
- [Paul] (chuckles) Certainly not.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.680
- Like, oh, what about
this, what about this?

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:47.640
What if we don't do this?

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:48.600
What if...

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:50.640
And Paul never makes you feel that way.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:55.640
Paul's excited by ideas, and
it's exhausting sometimes.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:56.730
I don't know how he does it,

00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:58.140
'cause actors can be a lot.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.690
So, it's really nice
when there's a sincerity

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:04.410
behind the collaborative nature
that he brings to the set.

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:05.430
- But I love it, I mean,

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:07.140
that's the only way I can work with women,

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:08.370
you know, I'm not like, you know,

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:11.510
like, I have some great
insight into women's stories

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:13.168
and how, you know, how everybody thinks,

00:02:13.168 --> 00:02:15.150
but I collaborate, you know.

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:16.267
That's like how, you know,

00:02:16.267 --> 00:02:18.210
here's what I want you to do.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:19.680
And they're like, "I don't
know if it would be like that."

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:20.790
Oh, cool, how is it?

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:22.890
So then we're a constant collaboration

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:23.970
and that's how it should always be.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:25.740
It should be men and
women working together.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:28.410
So we both avoid stereotyping
each other, both ways.

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:30.386
- We just found out how
you've been married so long.

00:02:30.386 --> 00:02:33.252
(audience claps)

00:02:33.252 --> 00:02:35.310
- And he's funny, which is always a plus.

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:36.390
He's so funny.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:38.070
He's just a natural ability.

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:42.503
So, making movies are long and tedious.

00:02:42.503 --> 00:02:46.110
And it's a real gift when
you get to work with people

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:47.040
that can make you laugh.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:47.873
- [Paul] Thank you.

00:02:47.873 --> 00:02:48.720
- Yeah. What about you?

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:50.130
Was it your first time working together?

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:50.963
- It was.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:50.963 --> 00:02:52.950
- It was, we'd been wanting to
work together for a long time

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:55.470
and we had developed
as producers together.

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:57.660
But I'd never acted with, or for, Paul

00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:58.920
and I was really excited.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:00.120
I'm such a huge fan.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:02.310
The first time I saw
Bridesmaids, I was like...

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:04.283
Crying, "Who is this director?

00:03:04.283 --> 00:03:07.336
How do I get to go to this party?"

00:03:07.336 --> 00:03:09.900
- And will anything ever
top the bathroom scene?

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:10.733
I don't know.

00:03:10.733 --> 00:03:11.566
- I don't know, I don't know.

00:03:11.566 --> 00:03:13.143
- I don't think so.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:14.970
- We need another break.

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:17.370
But I should also mention that
Paul has a book coming out

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:18.780
on November 15th.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:20.130
It's called Cocktail Time.

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:23.880
Great advice, embarrassing
stories, and 125 classic

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:24.750
and original drinks.

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:26.070
So y'all be sure to look for it.

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:28.050
Now it's time to meet the person who made

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:29.220
all of this possible.

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:31.673
He's the author of the School
for Good and Evil Books

00:03:31.673 --> 00:03:33.931
which have sold over 3 million copies

00:03:33.931 --> 00:03:36.344
and he's the executive
producer for the movie.

00:03:36.344 --> 00:03:38.299
Please welcome back to the show,

00:03:38.299 --> 00:03:39.649
Soman Chainani!

00:03:39.649 --> 00:03:41.253
(audience cheering)

00:03:41.253 --> 00:03:43.836
(upbeat music)

00:03:47.464 --> 00:03:50.464
(audience cheering)

00:03:59.122 --> 00:04:00.210
- [Kelly] It's like, I was like,

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:01.530
it's that game musical chair.

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:02.700
It keeps moving.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:05.130
So last time you were
here, we were talking

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:07.890
about this book and now
it's like a huge movie.

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:08.723
- It's crazy.

00:04:08.723 --> 00:04:09.582
(audience cheering)

00:04:09.582 --> 00:04:10.710
- [Kelly] It's so cool.

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:13.110
- It still feels like my mind exploded

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:15.090
and I haven't gotten all
the pieces back, you know.

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:16.560
So I feel a little bit jumbled.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.990
- Yeah. Is it just
amazing to have something

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:19.950
in your head and heart thing,

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:21.180
you've had these characters' looks

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:22.320
and then see it come to life?

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:25.890
Because, I believe it wasn't a
lot of CGI, it was like real.

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:27.303
- Yeah, we did as much
practical as we could.

00:04:27.303 --> 00:04:28.136
- [Kelly] Yeah!

00:04:28.136 --> 00:04:30.100
- And I think what happens
is when you're a writer,

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:32.790
your canvas is usually
people's imaginations.

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:34.937
So you're used to never actually seeing

00:04:34.937 --> 00:04:36.810
tangible results of your work.

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:38.207
You never see the world
actually built to life.

00:04:38.207 --> 00:04:39.040
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:40.282
- So to actually see it feels like

00:04:40.282 --> 00:04:41.700
you've won the lottery.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:43.230
It just seems like an extra bonus.

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:44.127
- Yeah.

00:04:44.127 --> 00:04:45.900
- And I just think the
entire experience of

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:48.420
especially this cast?
I mean, who gets this?

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:49.620
This is a once in a lifetime thing.

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:50.453
You know what I mean?

00:04:50.453 --> 00:04:51.334
- [Kelly] Yeah!

00:04:51.334 --> 00:04:53.720
It's an incredible chemistry.

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:55.920
So you even make a cameo in the movie.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:57.470
So look, look at this everybody.

00:04:57.470 --> 00:04:59.236
(Soman laughs)

00:04:59.236 --> 00:05:01.153
- [Kelly] Yes! So cool!

00:05:02.293 --> 00:05:04.140
I was like, was that for the one murder?

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:05.370
It was like, it was amazing.

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:06.339
It was like a teardrop.

00:05:06.339 --> 00:05:08.430
So everyone here is a fan of your book.

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:11.730
So why do you think this
series resonates so?

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:14.910
- You know, when I started
writing it, I was 28.

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:17.010
A lot of doors were closing in my life.

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:18.630
I felt like professionally, personally,

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:21.090
romantically, creatively,
so much rejection.

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:21.923
And I think...

00:05:21.923 --> 00:05:23.069
- [Kelly] Real break time. Wow.

00:05:23.069 --> 00:05:24.013
(Kelly laughs)

00:05:24.013 --> 00:05:26.220
- I just feel like people
have that moment in their life

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:28.230
where the universe is saying no to you.

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:30.870
And I felt like my path
was saying, "Change it up."

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:31.804
And I realized that

00:05:31.804 --> 00:05:35.340
I no longer could be the
person people wanted me to be

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:36.480
and I had to commit to just being...

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:37.313
- That's exhausting.

00:05:37.313 --> 00:05:38.146
- ...more on myself.

00:05:38.146 --> 00:05:38.979
- Yeah.

00:05:38.979 --> 00:05:40.020
- So, The School For
Good and Evil was like

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:42.450
a primal scream of my soul onto the page.

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:44.670
I was not in any way
thinking about success

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:45.870
or how it was gonna sell,

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:46.980
or what people were gonna think of it.

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:49.410
I just wanted to put
something that was all 100%

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:50.700
like me on there.

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:54.510
And the reason I think it's
resonated a lot with readers is

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:57.720
because we grew up being
told that if you're good,

00:05:57.720 --> 00:05:59.100
then you will have a happy ending.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:00.180
Everything's gonna open up for you.

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:01.860
You're gonna get the prince,
you're gonna get the princess.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.570
All you have to do is be good.

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:05.640
But most of us spend our
entire life every day

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:06.473
we wake up...

00:06:06.473 --> 00:06:07.350
- Growing that's a load of crap.

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:09.750
- Yeah. I don't feel good half the day.

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:10.860
Half the day I'm sort of cutting

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:11.940
in front of someone in the subway,

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:13.230
or like I'm accidentally like,

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:14.880
getting in the elevator
faster before someone else.

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:15.964
I'm pressing the button to close it.

00:06:15.964 --> 00:06:16.797
You know what I mean?

00:06:16.797 --> 00:06:17.630
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:19.434
- Like you're just constantly
doing little evil things.

00:06:19.434 --> 00:06:20.267
- [Paul] That was you?

00:06:20.267 --> 00:06:23.893
- [Kerry] Accidentally
closing the elevator?

00:06:23.893 --> 00:06:25.200
- Little good things, little evil things.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:26.520
And you're constantly going back and forth

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:28.710
between them and you
realize that we're never

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:30.270
ever gonna have the perfect happy ending.

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:31.530
We're not gonna have that ever after

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:33.960
because we're just trying
to be human every day.

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:35.580
We can't be good or pure evil.

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:36.413
We're, we're somewhere in between.

00:06:36.413 --> 00:06:38.100
- It was a cool revelation in the film.

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:39.870
I'm not gonna say who did it, but one

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:41.880
of these people on the
couch had that moment

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:43.980
in the movie and I was
like, Oh, that's so true.

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:45.900
I was like, we're all capable.

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:46.733
- Yeah.

00:06:46.733 --> 00:06:47.566
- Of both.

00:06:47.566 --> 00:06:49.320
- So Charlize, I understand
that you have to to run

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:50.880
but you have something
that you wanted to say

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:51.713
to our audience, right?

00:06:51.713 --> 00:06:54.870
- Yeah. Unfortunately we have
to go to the premier tonight.

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:55.703
- Yes.

00:06:55.703 --> 00:06:56.621
- Yeah.

00:06:56.621 --> 00:06:59.896
(audience cheers)

00:06:59.896 --> 00:07:02.640
- But it's just, I feel
like you, we can't waste

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:03.473
these outfits.

00:07:03.473 --> 00:07:04.770
You guys made such an effort.

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:05.625
So you should join us.

00:07:05.625 --> 00:07:06.689
- You should come.

00:07:06.689 --> 00:07:10.257
(audience cheers)

00:07:10.257 --> 00:07:11.798
- Everyone is the audience is headed

00:07:11.798 --> 00:07:13.809
with the cast to the world premiere

00:07:13.809 --> 00:07:15.908
of the "School for Good and Evil".

00:07:15.908 --> 00:07:17.396
- [Charlize] It's awesome.

00:07:17.396 --> 00:07:18.979
- Have so much fun.

00:07:20.562 --> 00:07:23.136
You can see the "School for
Good and Evil" on Netflix.

00:07:23.136 --> 00:07:25.240
Don't miss it. You're
gonna wanna watch it.

